{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>And after that, etc. — and it arose, etc.</b> One needs to know why it was necessary to say ״וְאַחֲרֵי כֵן״ (and after that), for it is self-understood from the sequence of events that the burial was afterward. Further, why was it necessary to say a second time ״וַיָּקָם הַשָּׂדֶה״ (and the field arose)?" "Indeed, the verse intends to inform us that the acquisition was carried out according to all the rules of acquisition, and that is its saying <b>וְאַחֲרֵי כֵן</b> (and after that) — meaning: after Abraham weighed out to Ephron the money for the cave, by this Ephron removed his rights from the land, as Rambam wrote in chapter 1 of the Laws of Acquisition and Gifts, and this is his language: a non-Jew, from the time he receives the money, removes his rights; but an Israelite does not acquire until a deed reaches his hand, and consequently these properties are like property of the wilderness, in which whoever takes possession acquires — end quote. You see that giving the money is effective in that no right remains to the non-Jew, and therefore it is effective to complete the acquisition into Abraham's hand by means of the act of possession (חֲזָקָה) that he took possession of it through burying Sarah in it. But had he buried Sarah beforehand — even though he would afterward give the money for the field — he would not have acquired by the giving of money alone, as explained, without an act of possession." "<b>And there remains</b> to know whether this act of possession (חֲזָקָה) is indeed called an act of possession regarding a non-Jew's land. Now, Rambam wrote in chapter 2 of the Laws of Acquisition and Gifts, and this is his language: But regarding ownerless property or that of a convert, even if one consumed produce for several years, he has acquired neither the trees nor the land until he performs an act upon the body of the earth, etc. How so? One who finds a palace among a convert's property or ownerless property and plasters it or raises [its wall] a handbreadth or more has acquired it, etc.; one who spreads mats has acquired, etc.; if a purchaser moved earth from a high place to a low place — behold, this repairs the land. If he was seen casting the earth without care, he is presumed to intend only to level a place for threshing, and he has not acquired — end quote. And the Magid Mishneh wrote: one who spreads mats — meaning, to sit upon them, and behold he derives benefit from the body of the land. Another interpretation — one who spreads mats: that he beautified the land by spreading them, like plastering and raising [walls], etc., and this is the primary interpretation, and with this the Rav ibn Migash, of blessed memory, agreed — end quote. Now, well according to the first interpretation of the Magid — that whoever derives benefit from the body of the land is called an act of possession regarding ownerless property — we may say that in our case as well, since he buried Sarah his wife in the body of the land, this counts as deriving benefit from the body of the land and he acquired it. But according to the opinion of the Rav ibn Migash — that it is not an act of possession unless he makes a physical improvement in the land such as plastering or raising [walls] — if so, Sarah's burial would not serve for the matter of possession, and how did Abraham acquire?" "And it is possible that when they construct the grave — and in particular the construction of the early tombs, which they would build as vaulted chambers (כּוּכִין) — they presumably plaster and raise [walls], and through this the act of possession (חֲזָקָה) would be effected. From this it will be resolved why the verse repeated a second time <b>וַיָּקָם הַשָּׂדֶה</b> (and the field arose): to say that after Abraham buried, etc., through this the acquisition (קִימָה) of the field to Abraham was completed. Without this, even though in Ephron's estimation his title was sealed in that he removed his rights, still it would not yet be called Abraham's according to the law as explained; but after he buried, etc. — ״וַיָּקָם״ (and it arose), etc."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 23, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:43:08.740233058Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}